The completion-counter rate: the busy block, and how many runtimes report --
trn1.2xlarge 2026-09-11 (rate_probe.py, which prints every sample's completions
summed over runtimes, then the row).

Two defects, both in the path that scores graph_replay:

1. The rate took the interior of the FIRST-TO-LAST busy period. Setup work
   completes before the timed loop with a compile between them, so the setup
   blip takes the "first" slot and the loop's partly busy first period stays in
   the average. whole_periods was fixed for exactly this on the flops and
   utilisation series (where it moved memory_read's utilisation from 88.7% to
   99.4%); the completion series still had it. It now uses the longest
   uninterrupted block, and the row carries both counts.

2. The series took one entry per runtime, not per sample. neuron-monitor
   reports every runtime on the device, so a second one -- a neuron-profile
   capture, an aggregate's worker -- put two entries at the same instant, and
   the rate divided one period's completions by two periods' worth of time.
   _completed_in and await_idle_period already summed over runtimes; the
   series now does too.

